My name is Gilad Golan.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.740
I'm engineering director for
security and trust in G Suite.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.892
And with me today is Nico.

00:00:12.892 --> 00:00:14.600
NICO LIDZBORSKI: Hi,
I'm Nico Lidzborski.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:20.720
I'm engineering lead for
security and trust on G Suite.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.550
GILAD GOLAN: And
today, we'd like

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.150
to talk about email security.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.240
So email-- we love it.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.420
We hate it.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.479
It clogs our inboxes.

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:36.740
It follows us around
on our phones,

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.190
but we can't pass
a day without it.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.030
Email has become the
lifeblood of any organization.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:50.470
So by latest estimates, there
are 3.7 billion email users.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:56.570
And every day, there are around
269 billion email messages

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.300
sent and received.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.200
And what made email so powerful?

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.150
So email is universal.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.610
It's open.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.640
It's fast, and it's free.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.520
So think about it.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.350
It's universal,
open, fast, and free.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.960
That's what makes
email so powerful,

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.960
but it's also what makes
it the most popular attack

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.930
vector for
cyberattacks, which is

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.510
why we want to spend
this talk today

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:34.220
discussing email security.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.690
So today, what we're
going to talk about,

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.000
we'll discuss some
of the latest threats

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.880
that we've been observing
related to email security.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.910
And we'll tell you what
Google does to protect you

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:51.420
against those threats.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.700
And then we'll finish by walking
you through some of the things

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.640
that you, as admin,
should do to take

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.025
advantage of the
protections that we provide.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.979
So these are the main
email-related threat categories

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:10.880
that we'll discuss today.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.360
So we're going to talk about
spam, phishing, malware,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.110
impersonation and interception.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:25.180
And we're also going to discuss
email client security and data

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.740
loss prevention as
it relates to email.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.450
And we're going to
start with spam,

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.900
which is the most common one.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.670
So spam is unsolicited,
irrelevant or inappropriate

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.270
messages sent over the
internet to a large number

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.950
of recipients.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.840
And we all know it.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.600
We all get it.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.820
We've almost become used to it.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.230
It's mostly commercial
advertising.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:58.870
It's often for dubious products,
all sorts of "get rich quick"

00:02:58.870 --> 00:02:59.960
schemes.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.590
The damage for
organization, it's

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.190
mostly waste of time
and productivity loss.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.480
But it does add up.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.000
So over time, it
can be significant.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.970
So what does Google do to
protect you against spam?

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:17.710
And to understand
what Google does,

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:22.930
we need to take a step back and
look at the progress of machine

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:30.890
learning in Google across all
Google products and services.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:34.585
So today, usage of
machine learning

00:03:34.585 --> 00:03:38.470
in all of Google products
has grown exponentially.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:42.940
And specifically, the latest
developments in deep learning

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:48.320
enable powerful new capabilities
not possible before that.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.180
What you see in this
graph is the number

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:58.340
of deep learning projects
across all of Google products.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.450
So you can see the huge growth
over the last couple of years.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.440
And the progress and the
shared infrastructure

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.010
that we build that powers
these innovations across all

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:18.320
of Google, they enable
us-- they give us

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.899
capabilities that
seem impossible

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:24.170
only a few years ago.

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:27.680
Just to give you an example
from a familiar product

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.920
of the kind of magic that
deep learning provides--

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.640
so this is how
Google Photos uses

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.990
deep learning models to analyze
photos and to autotag them.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.340
So using machine learning
and deep learning,

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.680
Google Photos can analyze
photos like the one

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.260
you see on the
left and determine

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.320
that this photo should be
tagged by the tag "ocean."

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:57.610
So let's get back to spam now.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:01.240
We take the similar
kind of magic

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:08.580
that you see in photo tagging
and apply it to spam detection.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:12.210
So in Google, for
spam detection,

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:15.450
we use what data
scientists call ensemble.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:19.560
Ensemble is when you take a
number of machine learning

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:27.200
models and use them combined
to achieve higher accuracy.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.460
So what you see in this diagram
is the different models that we

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:38.110
use and the kind of
spam detection accuracy

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:39.950
we achieve with that.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.250
So when you use
them in combination,

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:46.240
we achieve 99.9% accuracy.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.430
And we keep improving it.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:50.620
So what does it mean?

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:56.110
That means that we have less
than 0.1% false negatives.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:06:00.700
This is spam not detected,
adding up in your inboxes.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:05.410
And we have less than
0.05% false positives.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:09.580
This is mail by
mistake labeled as spam

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.980
and not showing in your inbox.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:15.250
By the way, the imbalance
that you see between the two

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:18.820
is by design because
false positives

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.450
are much more disruptive to
business than false negatives.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.260
So our models are
tuned and optimized

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.110
to minimize false positives.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:36.000
Now let's move on and
talk about phishing.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.150
So phishing is an
attempt to acquire

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.880
confidential information
by masquerading

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.370
to be a trustworthy
person or organization.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:49.600
The damage potential of
phishing is much higher.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.490
It can be both financial,
loss of intellectual property,

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:58.280
or damage to reputation.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:01.040
There is one especially
dangerous variant

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.980
of phishing, which
is spearfishing,

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:07.700
which is a targeted
message that appears

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.980
to come from a trusted person.

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:14.865
And that trusted person can
be your IT admin or maybe even

00:07:14.865 --> 00:07:15.365
your CEO.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:23.110
Let's look at an example
of a phishing attack.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.200
So a phishing attack
often appears like this--

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.820
a very innocent-looking
email message.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:33.930
In this case, it's directing
you to get some files

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.150
from Google Drive.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.870
Now what's interesting
is that often,

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:44.420
the link, the URL
in the message,

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:53.160
it is designed to appear like
a legitimate URL from Google.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:56.220
You see it's says
host.googledrive.com,

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:00.150
which would make you believe
it's a legitimate Google URL.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:01.520
Then it goes on--

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.990
.service.phish-something
and something.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.840
So it's just a
random URL designed

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.910
to look like
something from Google.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.980
And I'm just using
Google as an example.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.740
It could be your bank or
any other organization

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:14.385
that you trust.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.370
Now, if a user clicks
that link, that

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:28.190
will take you to a malicious
clone of a Google sign-in page.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:30.410
And this can be
designed to be a pixel

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:36.950
by pixel copy of the
real Google sign-in page.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:43.010
If the user unsuspectingly
enters their credential,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.590
those credentials immediately
go to the attackers.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.200
The attackers can
then immediately

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.530
use them to siphon off
sensitive data, which

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.980
can happen in seconds.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.650
And after that happens,
the damage is already done.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.510
So what does Google do to
protect you against phishing?

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:12.720
Before we go into the
specific mechanisms,

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.820
it's important to understand
that, as in many other cases,

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.270
we use a defense in-depth
approach, meaning

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:24.660
we have multiple
layers of defense where

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:30.090
we try to detect and reject
any phishing attacks.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:33.090
And if they go and pass
through undetected,

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:36.150
we have additional
layers of protection

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.880
against those phishing attacks.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.760
So let's look at the--

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:44.970
this is a list of some of
the detection mechanisms

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:46.350
that we have.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.740
So we have a detection
mechanism that

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:53.820
combines both rules written
by security analysts

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.310
with machine learning models.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:00.720
We have a real-time
reputation system,

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.010
which is a database maintained
in real-time that covers

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:09.120
domains, links, IP addresses.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:12.000
And the fact that,
in Gmail, we have

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.870
a user base of over a
billion users, that gives us

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:20.850
plenty of early signals
that can help us

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:25.380
maintain the database
up to date in real-time.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.140
And you as enterprise
customers, you

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:32.070
get the benefit
of that database,

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:38.370
which uses the usage pattern
we see from a billion users.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:46.950
All of these are signals
used for the early detection.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:50.930
We also do anomaly detection
on the various entities

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.180
in an email message.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:56.730
So we look at things
like, what's the To field,

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:03.960
the From field, and try to find
any anomalous patterns which

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.690
will, again, give
us a signal of,

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.070
this might be a phishing attack.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.700
We also look for
brand impersonation.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.860
So brand impersonation
would be, for example,

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.580
if you have an email
message that contains,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.240
let's say, the logo
of Bank of America

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.750
but it's actually not
from Bank of America.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:25.530
Again, that's a strong
signal that this

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:26.690
may be a phishing attack.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:33.090
So when we detect
a phishing attack,

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:36.300
we try to do it as early
as possible and reject it

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:40.080
so it doesn't even
get delivered.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.180
Now, what happens-- and
this sometimes does happen.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:50.490
If we detect a phishing attack
after the message was already

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:57.080
delivered, so when that
happens, what we do,

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:01.600
we go in and reclassify messages
that were already delivered.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.900
So if there's a new attack and
we just found out about it,

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:09.400
we're going to go and
see if this attack is

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:12.970
already present in messages
that were delivered.

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.850
And then we'll go
and reclassify it.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:19.900
And so it will have the
warning that you see there,

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:21.700
that big red warning.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:26.320
And the links in the message
itself will be disabled.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:29.260
And by the way, this
is a key difference

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:33.760
in the kind of protection that
you can get from a filtering

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:37.900
gateway solution and what
we at Google can give you,

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:42.280
because a filtering solution has
just one chance to protect you,

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:47.370
and that's when the email
is delivered to your server.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:51.130
After that, if they discover
something after that,

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:53.915
they cannot go back and
undeliver the message.

00:12:53.915 --> 00:12:56.960
We at Google, we can do it
throughout the whole life

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.765
cycle of an email message.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:06.460
Then we have another
last line of detection,

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:08.640
which is Safe Browsing.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.740
So Safe Browsing is
triggered every time

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:16.800
the user clicks a link.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.140
And then the link is
going to be checked

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:23.130
against a database of known
phishing and malware URLs.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:26.700
And if it's found to be in
the database, then instead

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:31.110
of going to that
web page, the user

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.920
is going to be presented with
this kind of warning telling

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.800
them that they're about
to do something very risky

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:40.935
and they should not proceed.

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:46.560
Safe Browsing is available
in Chrome as well as

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:47.970
Firefox and Safari.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:59.360
Now, so we have all these
powerful detection mechanisms,

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.010
but you may still ask--

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.650
and this is a great
question-- well,

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.920
what happens if
something manages

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:11.870
to go through undetected through
all those detection mechanisms?

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:17.000
Because it's an
asymmetric warfare.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.380
The attacker only
has to succeed once

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:25.280
to cause significant damage
to your organization.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:30.530
So we may be successful 99.9%
of the times, and one attack

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.380
can still be a huge damage
to your organization.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:37.990
So we need some
last line of defense

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:41.100
to protect against the
attack that did go through.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:47.890
And this is your
line of defense--

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:50.110
the security keys.

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:53.980
So these are
cryptographically secure,

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:58.140
second-factor
authentication which

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.000
are not vulnerable to phishing.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:05.010
They're based on industry
standard FIDO UTF.

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:10.440
They support both USB for
use with laptops and desktops

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:11.790
as well as NFC.

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:14.800
So they can be used
with mobile devices.

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:16.830
And they are secure.

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:20.095
Even if an attacker
gains access,

00:15:20.095 --> 00:15:23.280
gains the credential
for an account,

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.580
the attacker cannot log into
that account because they need

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:29.880
physical possession
of the security keys.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.190
You actually have to
touch, physically touch,

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:35.205
the security key to gain access.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:40.900
These keys are simple to use.

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:45.640
And as an organization, you can
enforce the usage of the keys

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:50.110
through the G Suite
admin console.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:54.020
In Google, everybody
uses security keys.

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:56.500
You have to have
security keys in order

00:15:56.500 --> 00:16:02.470
to log into any internal
Google corporate asset.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:06.790
And it's been working remarkably
well for us at Google.

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:14.200
Let's move onto the
next threat, malware.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:19.300
So if you've been following
the trends in malware

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:25.090
for the last 10 or 15 years,
you could see a big change

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:29.470
from the first
internet-scale attacks

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:34.240
that we saw in the early 2000s
and the ones that we see today.

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:37.600
The malware of the
early 2000, they

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:43.330
were designed mostly for it
to cause some random damage.

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:48.070
And they were designed
primarily for fast propagation.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:50.800
So they just want to
spread as fast as possible

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:53.500
before the anti-virus
develops signatures

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.410
and can detect and block them.

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:59.780
The ones we're seeing
today are very different.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:02.440
They're much more sophisticated.

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:04.569
They're financially motivated.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:07.599
So it's typically
part of a crime ring.

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:10.369
And it can be very profitable.

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:12.069
They're targeted.

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:14.230
There are stealth.

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:16.540
And they use a number
of countermeasures

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:19.680
to avoid detection.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.380
And now I'm going to
ask Nico to walk us

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.359
through an example
of such malware

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:27.750
that demonstrates all
the recent trends.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:33.120
NICO LIDZBORSKI: So we
are going to go today

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:37.410
over the case of Locky malware.

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:40.640
So starting in
February 2016, people

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:43.700
started being targeted
by that malware.

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:46.760
So Locky is ransomware.

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:50.600
It uses encryption to
prevent access to files

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:53.510
until your ransom is paid for.

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:57.020
We estimate that 100 million
people have been targeted

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.390
by that particular malware.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:03.740
It appeared in multiple variants
and auto-released new versions

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:07.400
to counter defenses
that are in place.

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:12.130
So targeting a victim have
been slowly evolving over time.

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:14.280
For instance, the victims
of prior data breaches

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:17.350
[INAUDIBLE] OPM have been
particularly targeted

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:20.590
by that malware
when it came out.

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:23.740
And ransomeware is really a
booming business with profits

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:24.890
in the millions of dollars.

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:33.910
So how does Locky come
up into your inbox?

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:37.700
So it's simply delivered as
a simple email attachment.

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:41.040
It appears here as a simple
invoice in a Word document.

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:46.200
So Locky is using
social engineering

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:51.590
to bypass default security
settings and executing macros.

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:58.060
So with this very advanced text,
it's taking advantage of users.

00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:00.210
So the [INAUDIBLE]
here is your protection

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:02.380
is only as strong as
your weakest link.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:05.410
And the weakest link
is often the user.

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:08.630
So even an attack with a
high level of sophistication

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:11.210
needs social engineering here.

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.530
And now Locky is able to run
the next stage of its attack

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:15.992
based on scripting.

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:23.850
So we're going to go into
some of the detail of Locky.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:27.460
So Locky knows that
modern malware scanners

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:32.950
are using sandbox to monitor
behavior of suspect files.

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:35.860
So sophisticated
malware like Locky

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:40.180
are using tricks to detect
monitoring inside the sandbox.

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:43.450
So here, you can see an example
of what Locky has been doing.

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:46.359
They are keeping track of
time to detect emulation

00:19:46.359 --> 00:19:46.900
in a sandbox.

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:54.820
So they start by checking the
time, sleeping for some time.

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:56.530
And sandbox typically
do not really

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:59.290
sleep because people who are
writing sandbox [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:59.290 --> 00:19:59.890
be efficient.

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:03.040
They don't want to be
like spinning idlessly.

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:05.330
And then Locky's able
to compute the time

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:08.182
difference and [INAUDIBLE]
it was running in sandbox.

00:20:08.182 --> 00:20:09.640
And if it's running
in the sandbox,

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:13.060
it will abort early and not
have any malicious payload.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:22.370
Now, Locky's also
targeting specific OSes.

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:26.210
So it uses these subtle
difference in script engines

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:28.550
to detect a specific target.

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:30.800
If not on the
appropriate target,

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.890
it's going to remain
dormant and avoid detection.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:36.170
So here you can see
a snippet showing you

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:39.260
how Locky only wants to
use a Microsoft Jscript

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:41.950
interpreter, not JavaScript.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:44.720
It's just checking
for specific syntax

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.630
that only work in JScript
and uses that to move along.

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:53.980
So once a target
has been identified,

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:57.240
Locky moves to the next
stage and really attacks.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:01.720
It's going to download,
install, and execute

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:02.740
the malicious binary.

00:21:06.620 --> 00:21:09.260
Of course, this
was simplification.

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:11.800
The actual payload is
extremely obfuscated

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:13.610
in order to run a lot
of advanced services

00:21:13.610 --> 00:21:16.300
to de-obsfucate all the content.

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:22.540
So when the attack
code is executed,

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:27.620
[INAUDIBLE] all user documents
get strongly encrypted.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:30.800
Locky is using actually
like secure encryption

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:35.510
to ensure all the
payload is locked down.

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:38.070
As a ransomware,
Locky is supported

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:40.890
by a full e-commerce operation.

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:44.570
So they host a
decrypter website on Tor

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:48.180
that are very resilient
and untraceable.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:50.970
They also ask for ransom
to be paid in bitcoin

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:52.298
to be untraceable.

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:59.420
So now, how are we
tackling malware in Gmail?

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:02.360
So overall, we take
a funnel approach

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:06.660
starting with blank
rejection of common vectors

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:11.090
with known high-risk format
where we actually just blanket

00:22:11.090 --> 00:22:14.690
blocking at the
entrance level of Gmail.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:18.070
And we also have a
diversified approach

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:21.410
that uses multiple file
scanning technologies.

00:22:21.410 --> 00:22:26.810
We use internal scanner as
well as external AV partners.

00:22:26.810 --> 00:22:29.660
We have intelligent
attachment scanning.

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:33.140
It starts with signature
base, but we support deep

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:36.950
scanning for risky
attachment style.

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:39.290
So risky attachment
get deep scanning,

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:43.310
which means the attachment
actually opens inside a sandbox

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:44.580
environment.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.970
And we monitor
malicious behavior

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:49.560
to detect something
is amok and consider

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:52.980
that piece of software malware
without relying entirely

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:55.080
on signature here.

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:57.470
So we have a real-time
continuous update

00:22:57.470 --> 00:22:59.780
on malicious content.

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:03.060
We are also doing rescanning
of content at download time.

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:05.960
So even at the last moment
when you click Download,

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:08.420
it's actually going
to verify again

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:13.040
whether the definition has
been updated since then.

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:17.400
Finally, we also do link
scanning for malware detection.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:21.530
We are going over
[INAUDIBLE] list of links

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:23.120
that we know are
malicious, and we

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:26.750
prevent them to be downloaded.

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:31.010
So once again, after all the
defenses could potentially

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:33.200
fail, we still
have Safe Browsing

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:38.970
that constantly updates
blacklist of suspicious sites.

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:42.080
So Safe Browsing is taking
care of leftover malware

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:45.020
and phishing that potentially
was not detected earlier.

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:51.390
GILAD GOLAN: Thanks, Nico.

00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:55.200
So we covered so far
the three big threats

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:59.130
related to email, which are
spam, phishing and malware.

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:01.430
Now let's move on and talk
about the impersonation

00:24:01.430 --> 00:24:03.690
and interception.

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:06.390
And before we talk
about this, it's

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:10.680
important to understand the root
cause that these threats even

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:11.880
exist.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:14.820
And the root cause is SMTP.

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:18.450
So SMTP, as you may
know, that's the protocol

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:22.290
used to send email
across the internet.

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:25.310
It's one of the earliest
internet protocols.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:29.970
And SMTP stands for Simple
Mail Transport Protocol.

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:33.360
And as the name
implies, it is simple.

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:39.480
It was designed for a safe
and trusted environment.

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:41.760
Threats were not
taken into account.

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:46.440
And therefore, SMTP, the
original SMTP has no security

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:50.030
mechanisms whatsoever,
which is why

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:53.450
we have to deal with
these threats today.

00:24:53.450 --> 00:24:55.160
So what are they?

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:58.040
So impersonation
is when an attacker

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:03.000
sends email appearing to be
from your domain or from one

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:06.540
or some of your domain users.

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:10.700
Interception is when an
attacker gains access

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:14.690
to email content in
transit, typically

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:18.710
by some "man in the middle"
attack, which is important

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:22.160
because SMTP traffic
typically travels

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:25.552
over multiple hops between
source and destination.

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:32.230
And I'll let Nico walk us
through some of the mechanisms

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:38.680
that we have to deal with
interception and impersonation.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:42.920
NICO LIDZBORSKI: So first
of all, for impersonation

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:47.630
prevention, we rely on industry
standards that add security

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:50.430
on top of SMTP protocol.

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:54.140
All the standards are taking
advantage of DNS record

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:56.530
with a standard domain to
publish security settings

00:25:56.530 --> 00:25:59.130
associated with the domain.

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:03.170
So we have SPF, which are
performing authentication

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:06.380
by specifying what
are the email servers

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:09.920
to trust DKIM doing
authentication

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:13.370
using cryptographic
signatures and DMARC,

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:16.710
which establishes a policy on
how to deal with authentication

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:17.210
failures.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:23.770
Well, first, SPF
is fairly simple.

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:26.450
It's registering the IP
address of mail server

00:26:26.450 --> 00:26:30.160
that are authorized to
send mail from a domain.

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:34.020
So here, we have an example
of the policy for a google.com

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:39.905
domain which lists the known
IPs all our outbound gateways.

00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:49.910
So we have observed, though,
several shortcomings with SPF.

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:51.560
First, invalid records.

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:53.610
People are putting
invalid records in DNS.

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:55.526
They don't realize that
they don't necessarily

00:26:55.526 --> 00:26:56.660
parse correctly.

00:26:56.660 --> 00:26:57.950
And they cannot be parsed.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:00.320
Therefore, we are unable to
make a determination on what

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:02.900
the policy actually means.

00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:07.250
The record can also be
white-listing large IP ranges.

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:10.250
And those IP ranges may
fall outside the domain

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:12.590
or allow undesirable
hosts to send emails.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:19.940
Systems that are sharing public
IP pools with different senders

00:27:19.940 --> 00:27:24.050
are also vulnerable
to that type of issue.

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:27.290
Then BGP hijacking
have enabled attackers

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:31.100
to quickly use priorly
white-listed IPs

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:33.510
to send messages.

00:27:33.510 --> 00:27:35.960
And finally, DNS records
are very important.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.580
And they can be hijacked, where
the attacker will actually

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:42.070
insert their own IP that
authorizes IPs to send emails.

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:49.640
So DKIM [INAUDIBLE] offering
cryptographic signing of

00:27:49.640 --> 00:27:53.800
sent emails using
specific pair domain keys.

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:59.400
So this example
of a message, you

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:04.500
can see the DKIM header,
which is configured

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:08.370
to target specific headers
inside the message that

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:12.480
give message ID, date,
subject, from, to.

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:17.040
So those headers as well
as the body of the message

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:20.340
are in scope for hashing.

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:23.460
So cryptographic
hashing is done.

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:28.200
That has is added as
part of the DKIM record.

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:34.720
And that harsh is signed
using the specific domain key.

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:38.280
Now, how is that
signature verified?

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:41.550
When the email is
received, the DKIM header

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:46.630
includes a selector on which
key to fetch over in DNS.

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:49.440
That key is fetched.

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:52.470
And then it is used to
verify the signature.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:56.190
So we get cryptographic proof
that the content of the message

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:59.550
hasn't been tampered with.

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:02.680
However, DKIM also
has shortcomings.

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:05.740
It provides strong
improvement over SPF,

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:08.110
but standard is
much more complex.

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:12.220
It requires cryptography
and several libraries

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:15.010
that try to implement
the standard actually

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:15.820
had flaws in it.

00:29:15.820 --> 00:29:18.020
They did not verify everything.

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:21.160
So it was actually abused.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:25.000
Weak keys, like 512
bit [INAUDIBLE],

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.280
had been using DKIM.

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:29.710
And attackers have been
able to crack those keys

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:32.880
and generate the private
key associated with it

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:35.360
and are able to sign
on behalf of a domain.

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:38.800
So we don't trust
weak keys anymore.

00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:42.520
Private key can also be stolen
from compromised mail server.

00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:44.920
So misconfigured server
that allows an attacker

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:48.790
to breach it, the attacker
can steal the DKIM key

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:53.220
and can sign in a
impersonating the domain.

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:55.940
Finally, again,
it relies on DNS.

00:29:55.940 --> 00:29:58.590
So hijacked DNS record
have been used in the past

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:00.400
to insert malicious DKIM keys.

00:30:04.910 --> 00:30:08.110
DMARC is offering
a policy framework

00:30:08.110 --> 00:30:12.940
that defines the action to take
upon failure of DKIM and SPF.

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:16.340
Without it, the
interpretation of DKIM and SPF

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:20.300
is really left to the
recipient's mail server.

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:25.940
So another feature of
DMARC is also reporting.

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:29.520
Reporting allows aggregate
visibility of attempts

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:30.390
to attack a domain.

00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:36.434
So we see example for
a google.com record.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.390
You can see the policy
is set to reject

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:45.510
messages failing DKIM and SPF.

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:51.430
It also requires the server
to send an aggregate email

00:30:51.430 --> 00:30:56.450
with a list of failures
so the sender domain

00:30:56.450 --> 00:31:00.369
impersonated is able to
figure out what's happening.

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:08.330
So we have taken steps to
surface authentication status

00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:10.220
to end users.

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:14.300
The goal is really to raise user
awareness of possible phishing

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:16.550
attacks.

00:31:16.550 --> 00:31:19.190
We have a fairly clear
visual difference set

00:31:19.190 --> 00:31:23.780
between the left side with
an authenticated email that

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:27.510
carries the logo for the
domain, and the right side,

00:31:27.510 --> 00:31:30.130
which is not authenticated
with an appropriate symbol.

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:38.530
Overall, we observe a really
steady progress towards better

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:40.540
email authentication.

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:47.190
So non-authenticated email is
now at around 1.8% late 2016,

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:51.140
down from 5.8% two years ago.

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:55.520
Dual authentication
with both SPF and DKIM

00:31:55.520 --> 00:32:01.910
is now at like 89.1%, up
from 80.8% two years ago.

00:32:01.910 --> 00:32:04.630
So we are really
getting towards a world

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:08.470
where all unauthenticated
messages are really associated

00:32:08.470 --> 00:32:10.433
with something very malicious.

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:16.320
Now we'd like to go over
another threat against email

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:19.022
but is interception.

00:32:19.022 --> 00:32:20.480
At this point, I'd
like to give you

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:25.340
a quick primer on
35-year-old SMTP protocol.

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:28.730
So in standard SMTP,
client basically

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:33.380
connects to server over TCP
and assemble the email line

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:35.126
by line.

00:32:35.126 --> 00:32:39.790
In traditional SMTP, all
that is going over clear text

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:42.420
where server and client just
communicate with each other.

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:48.880
Now, if we look at the
network perspective for this,

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:52.330
the email message is transiting
between Alice's sever and Bob's

00:32:52.330 --> 00:32:55.290
sever over the internet.

00:32:55.290 --> 00:33:00.210
So an eavesdropper can
trivially intercept any message.

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:02.840
It doesn't have to be active.

00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:05.870
It's just passive attacker
just listening over the wire

00:33:05.870 --> 00:33:07.390
and be able to see the messages.

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:15.130
SMTP encryption came as
an afterthought in 1999

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:18.520
and was therefore
added as opportunistic,

00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:21.280
which means it's best effort.

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:23.920
It requires both client
and server to support it.

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:29.000
In this case, you
can see the beginning

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:33.430
of an SMTP transaction
that supports STARTTLS.

00:33:33.430 --> 00:33:38.330
And the sever advertises it's
ability to support STARTTLS.

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:43.520
So the client then establishes
a full TLS connection.

00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:47.330
And inside that TLS
connection that's encrypted,

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:51.456
the actual content of the email
can be transmitted securely.

00:33:54.790 --> 00:33:56.920
Back to our network
perspective--

00:33:56.920 --> 00:34:01.590
so messaging or transiting
over a TLS-encrypted channel

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:05.630
has the consequent eavesdropper
is unable to passively observe

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:06.490
the message content.

00:34:10.330 --> 00:34:14.290
So in the last
year, we've really

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:17.830
taken to raise
end user awareness

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:21.070
on the security of communication
of various parties.

00:34:21.070 --> 00:34:25.600
So we introduced a broken lock
UI specifically simply showing

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:29.000
to users whenever a
received email was received

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:31.570
over a clear text
connection rather than

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:35.719
a safe, encrypted connection.

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:37.690
With that user
awareness, we have

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:39.670
been able to drive adoption.

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:43.150
Users started to ask senders
why their email were not

00:34:43.150 --> 00:34:44.585
encrypted--

00:34:44.585 --> 00:34:45.900
starting with their banks.

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:47.420
I received an
email from my bank.

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:49.560
Why is it not encrypted?

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:54.100
So [INAUDIBLE] we saw
incoming email encryption rate

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:58.370
go from the upper 50s to 82%.

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:01.760
So surfacing encryption
status to end user

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:04.010
was enough to put pressure
and drive encryption

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:07.490
use, a very big win
for the internet

00:35:07.490 --> 00:35:10.430
because billions more messages
are encrypted every day because

00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:11.721
of that [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:17.800
The current status at
Gmail is of course,

00:35:17.800 --> 00:35:22.740
all messages between Gmail
users are encrypted as always.

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:28.170
But 82% of inbound emails from
external senders are encrypted,

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:31.350
and 88% of outbound email
leaving Gmail service

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:32.806
are encrypted.

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:40.450
STARTTLS is not perfect, though.

00:35:40.450 --> 00:35:42.760
Email messages can
travel multiple hops

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:44.840
between sender and recipient.

00:35:44.840 --> 00:35:51.110
The STARTTLS is only
hop-by-hop protection.

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:54.610
As a consequence, eavesdropper
can still intercept email

00:35:54.610 --> 00:35:56.240
if there's an unencrypted hop.

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:04.100
So solution for this
was to actually try

00:36:04.100 --> 00:36:07.520
to protect the message
from the sender all

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:09.300
the way to the recipient.

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:11.920
So in January of this year,
we added Hosted S/MIME

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:17.510
support inside Gmail available
G suite enterprise customers.

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:19.700
So S/MIME is an
industry standard

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:22.400
for encrypting and
signing messages

00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:25.430
with user-specific keys.

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:29.180
S/MIME is secure, but key
management had traditionally

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:30.800
made it very
difficult to implement

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:33.170
at scale in organizations.

00:36:33.170 --> 00:36:35.610
So we've hosted a
S/MIME support in Gmail.

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:40.970
Company's domain administrator
can upload user certificate

00:36:40.970 --> 00:36:44.990
and just simply use S/MIME
to sign and encrypt messages

00:36:44.990 --> 00:36:51.390
whenever possible and
keep eavesdropper out.

00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:57.110
So S/MIME is not just
providing encryption.

00:36:57.110 --> 00:37:01.000
It's also signing each
message with the sender's key,

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:03.590
protecting against
impersonation and guarantying

00:37:03.590 --> 00:37:04.840
message integrity.

00:37:07.660 --> 00:37:11.410
Once rolled out, hosted
S/MIME requires no end user

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:13.540
involvement, which
is a big challenge

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:15.870
with the solution of the past.

00:37:15.870 --> 00:37:18.910
End users don't have to
deal with the complexities

00:37:18.910 --> 00:37:20.530
of managing certificate
and applying

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:21.941
the encryption themselves.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:28.290
So now we're going to go
over a different topic, which

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:29.510
is client safety.

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:35.870
Google is constantly
investing in providing you

00:37:35.870 --> 00:37:38.580
with the greatest innovation.

00:37:38.580 --> 00:37:40.640
And Gmail clients are
taking advantage of this

00:37:40.640 --> 00:37:44.510
to provide you with
a safe experience.

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:48.000
So then the email
is based on HTML,

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:51.260
enabling very rich content,
like beautiful image

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:53.400
inside messages.

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:56.300
However, it also
introduces risk.

00:37:56.300 --> 00:38:00.110
So we are now going to dive into
some of the innards of Gmail

00:38:00.110 --> 00:38:02.780
that are making
this safe for users.

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:12.660
So HTML in email messages is
coming from third parties.

00:38:12.660 --> 00:38:16.390
So we don't have control
over third parties.

00:38:16.390 --> 00:38:19.670
Therefore, potentially, content
can be potentially broken

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:22.400
or contain unsafe elements.

00:38:22.400 --> 00:38:25.600
As a consequence, Gmail team
is walking on state-of-the-art

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:29.780
sanitizer that fixes HTML
and removes unsafe elements.

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:37.490
So in this example
of HTML content,

00:38:37.490 --> 00:38:42.500
I highlighted some of the
content that needs to be fixed.

00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:46.910
Like an example is the margin
in the case it's invalid,

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:50.120
potentially going negative,
going outside of a window,

00:38:50.120 --> 00:38:53.720
and enabling click
hijacking, overriding

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:55.970
some element of the UI.

00:38:55.970 --> 00:38:58.250
The bold tag is
not balanced, which

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:01.290
can now allow escaping
outside of HTML content.

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:04.970
The rest of the message
is not balanced as well.

00:39:04.970 --> 00:39:07.490
So list should
always be enclosed.

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:10.310
The link URL should
be fully qualified

00:39:10.310 --> 00:39:15.740
and go through a redirector,
typically to hide the referrer.

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:18.530
And finally, of course,
we have a script

00:39:18.530 --> 00:39:21.980
that should be removed to
prevent [INAUDIBLE] scripting

00:39:21.980 --> 00:39:23.280
against your email.

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:27.990
So after going
through sanitization,

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:30.840
the HTML document
has been fixed and is

00:39:30.840 --> 00:39:32.810
safe to display
within Gmail clients.

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:41.350
Now, for images,
starting in 2013,

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:44.950
Gmail started proxying
all external images.

00:39:44.950 --> 00:39:47.650
So we are doing
this to protect you

00:39:47.650 --> 00:39:50.650
from senders who might
try to use images

00:39:50.650 --> 00:39:55.380
to compromise the security of
your computer or mobile device.

00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:57.650
So all images in
Gmail are loaded

00:39:57.650 --> 00:40:00.400
through Google proxy service.

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:05.360
They are delivered to
clients over HTTPS only.

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:10.130
They prevent leaking of
IP address or cookies.

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:12.530
And they also prevent
malicious images formats.

00:40:15.370 --> 00:40:18.060
So this, of course, is just
a subset of the security

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:20.610
capabilities we have
been able to provide

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:21.760
to you through our clients.

00:40:25.218 --> 00:40:27.690
GILAD GOLAN: Thanks, Nico.

00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:32.150
So so far, we talked about
different threats and attack

00:40:32.150 --> 00:40:36.590
vectors that come from
outside the organization

00:40:36.590 --> 00:40:41.690
and they pose risk
to internal assets.

00:40:41.690 --> 00:40:44.190
But think about the
other way around.

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:51.050
Email can also be used
for data exfiltration.

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:55.130
So everybody has
sensitive information

00:40:55.130 --> 00:40:56.750
inside the organization.

00:40:56.750 --> 00:41:01.100
Email can be used for that
data to leave the organization

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:03.330
and end up in the wrong hands.

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:07.190
And that can be either
negligent or malicious.

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:13.430
So at Google, we provide
DLP, Data Loss Protections,

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:17.150
to prevent such leakage.

00:41:17.150 --> 00:41:20.680
And I'm not going to go through
all the different options

00:41:20.680 --> 00:41:25.940
that you have in
setting DLP policy.

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:29.700
There's a fairly rich set
of rules and capabilities

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:30.920
that you can define.

00:41:30.920 --> 00:41:32.950
This is just one example.

00:41:32.950 --> 00:41:35.460
So here, you see
defining a rule.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:38.377
The rule is going to be applied
to all outbound messages,

00:41:38.377 --> 00:41:39.960
anything that leaves
your organization

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:42.830
and goes to somebody
outside the organization.

00:41:42.830 --> 00:41:45.320
In this case, the
rule would be applied

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:50.030
to any message that contains
a social security number.

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:53.720
And so in the rules
that we offer,

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:56.810
there is a large number
of pre-defined matches

00:41:56.810 --> 00:42:01.820
for sensitive data, such as
social security number, as well

00:42:01.820 --> 00:42:06.560
as the ability to define
your own custom matches

00:42:06.560 --> 00:42:10.310
for any sensitive data which
is proprietary and unique

00:42:10.310 --> 00:42:12.800
to your organization.

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:14.360
As part of the
rule, you can also

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:18.320
define what we should
do when we have a match.

00:42:18.320 --> 00:42:21.560
So we can either
modify the message,

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:23.250
reject it, or quarantine it.

00:42:27.350 --> 00:42:32.450
So to summarize, so
today, we gave you

00:42:32.450 --> 00:42:37.680
a picture of some
of the protections

00:42:37.680 --> 00:42:42.720
that we've built around
your usage of email

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:47.160
in Gmail and G Suite.

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:51.240
And this is an area that
is very important for us,

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:54.780
and we're going to
keep investing in that

00:42:54.780 --> 00:42:58.170
and improve those protections.

00:42:58.170 --> 00:43:01.950
But you have to keep
in mind that this is--

00:43:01.950 --> 00:43:05.400
for your domain, this is
a shared responsibility.

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:09.270
We're going to keep
improving those protections,

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:11.020
the mechanism.

00:43:11.020 --> 00:43:13.650
But to really keep
your domain safe,

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:16.810
you have to take
advantage of them.

00:43:16.810 --> 00:43:21.240
So before we end,
I just want to walk

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:25.020
through some of the
things, some of the actions

00:43:25.020 --> 00:43:27.840
that we recommend
that you take in order

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:30.145
to keep your domain safe.

00:43:34.720 --> 00:43:42.070
So the first one is
to use security keys

00:43:42.070 --> 00:43:45.850
for authentication, for
second-factor authentication.

00:43:45.850 --> 00:43:49.520
And I cannot
emphasize this enough.

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:52.180
This is your best
line of protections

00:43:52.180 --> 00:43:55.090
against phishing attack.

00:43:55.090 --> 00:43:59.800
If you use security
keys and force

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:06.020
that in your organization,
your security posture,

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:07.840
when it comes to
phishing, is going

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:10.130
to be an order of
magnitude better.

00:44:14.050 --> 00:44:19.350
Next, so as Nico
described earlier,

00:44:19.350 --> 00:44:23.800
SMTP is inherently
not a safe protocol.

00:44:23.800 --> 00:44:26.590
So you should set
up DKIM and SPF.

00:44:26.590 --> 00:44:31.330
We support both DKIM and
SBF in Gmail and G Suite,

00:44:31.330 --> 00:44:32.980
but you have to set it up.

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:37.570
So go to your admin
console and make sure

00:44:37.570 --> 00:44:42.520
that it is set up and
properly configured.

00:44:42.520 --> 00:44:45.490
And remember-- so
DKIM and SPF is used

00:44:45.490 --> 00:44:47.140
to authenticate your email.

00:44:47.140 --> 00:44:51.490
So you basically
tell the world, these

00:44:51.490 --> 00:44:55.840
are the servers that are allowed
to send email on my behalf,

00:44:55.840 --> 00:44:59.800
and this is how I sign email
messages to authenticate

00:44:59.800 --> 00:45:01.461
that these really came from me.

00:45:04.690 --> 00:45:09.610
Once you set up and configure
DKIM and SPF, the next step,

00:45:09.610 --> 00:45:12.580
you should go ahead and
set up a DMARC policy.

00:45:12.580 --> 00:45:17.080
A DMARC policy essentially,
after you did the first step

00:45:17.080 --> 00:45:22.840
saying, hey, world, this is how
I send email, the next step is

00:45:22.840 --> 00:45:24.760
to tell the world,
this is what you should

00:45:24.760 --> 00:45:30.340
do if you get email that
appears to come from me

00:45:30.340 --> 00:45:32.950
but it fails those
authentication mechanism,

00:45:32.950 --> 00:45:35.880
like the signature
doesn't match up.

00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:40.180
It's not one of the servers that
I specified in my SPF settings,

00:45:40.180 --> 00:45:41.540
what should you do?

00:45:41.540 --> 00:45:44.320
So I can tell the world,
if it appears that way,

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:48.160
reject it or report to me,
and this is the email address.

00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:50.260
So you can tell the
rest of the world

00:45:50.260 --> 00:45:53.560
how to deal with something
that comes from your domain

00:45:53.560 --> 00:45:56.080
and fails those security checks.

00:45:56.080 --> 00:45:58.870
It's a critical
step to make sure

00:45:58.870 --> 00:46:03.010
that your domain doesn't become
a source of phishing and spam

00:46:03.010 --> 00:46:06.850
if any machine by one of
your users gets compromised.

00:46:10.870 --> 00:46:13.870
The next one is use S/MIME.

00:46:13.870 --> 00:46:20.750
S/MIME gives you the best level
of security and encryption

00:46:20.750 --> 00:46:22.300
for email messages.

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:27.850
That means every
message is going

00:46:27.850 --> 00:46:33.220
to be both signed and encrypted
with a user-specific key,

00:46:33.220 --> 00:46:36.370
and it's going to be encrypted
through all the hops it

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:41.258
goes between sending server
and receiving server.

00:46:45.380 --> 00:46:48.800
Next, set up DLP rules.

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:49.730
You have it.

00:46:49.730 --> 00:46:50.390
It's there.

00:46:50.390 --> 00:46:52.340
You just have to set it up.

00:46:52.340 --> 00:46:55.310
Every organization
has and deals with

00:46:55.310 --> 00:46:57.920
some sensitive information.

00:46:57.920 --> 00:47:03.980
That can be social security
number, bank routing numbers,

00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:05.780
license driving numbers.

00:47:05.780 --> 00:47:08.030
Whatever it is,
make sure, if you

00:47:08.030 --> 00:47:10.790
don't want that to
leave the organization

00:47:10.790 --> 00:47:14.000
and end up in the wrong
hands, do something about it.

00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:15.590
You can monitor it.

00:47:15.590 --> 00:47:16.760
You can reject it.

00:47:16.760 --> 00:47:18.656
You can set a policy
that enforces it.

00:47:22.060 --> 00:47:28.600
And the last one is encourage
users to use safe clients

00:47:28.600 --> 00:47:29.830
when they use Gmail.

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:34.870
So as Nico showed you, we
make a tremendous effort

00:47:34.870 --> 00:47:41.080
in making sure that we sanitize
the content that we present

00:47:41.080 --> 00:47:45.010
to you in our email clients,
whether that's the web

00:47:45.010 --> 00:47:49.160
client, or Android, or iOS.

00:47:49.160 --> 00:47:50.365
So take advantage of that.

00:47:53.650 --> 00:47:55.900
Your users' email
usage is going to be

00:47:55.900 --> 00:47:59.950
much safer if they use
these clients than if they

00:47:59.950 --> 00:48:01.237
use a third party client.

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:12.900
And last but not least, as
we've seen in the Locky example,

00:48:12.900 --> 00:48:15.750
the best security
can be defeated

00:48:15.750 --> 00:48:20.150
by exploiting human weaknesses.

00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:26.470
So educate your users to
use email responsibly,

00:48:26.470 --> 00:48:29.980
avoid risky behaviors,
and pay attention

00:48:29.980 --> 00:48:32.410
to all the signals
and the warnings

00:48:32.410 --> 00:48:36.520
that we provide to make sure
that your organization stays

00:48:36.520 --> 00:48:37.420
safe.

00:48:37.420 --> 00:48:40.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

